Date: Wed, 3 Feb 93 23:03:06 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: PPL: What is a PPL, anyway?

I've noted before that any of the present governments could be
duplicated (within a perfect libertarian culture) with a suitable
contract -- after all, one can contract away one's labor, one's
property, even one's life.  (Of course, finding people to sign the
contract might be harder...)  But nobody is willing to recognize such
entities as PPLs, even though they would be private, and they whould
contain legal systems.

Why?

It seems to me that all of the PPLs that people have discussed here
share certain elements that are not inherent in contracted legal
systems, but are critical to what people *really mean* when they say
"PPL".  That element is the practical ability to "opt out" of the
system at any time, taking with one's self one's property, real
estate, business, etc.

OK, that probably doesn't surprise anybody.  But it makes a PPL a
pretty interesting beast.  Even road-maintenance cooperatives haven't
mastered how to allow a single householder in the middle of the
cooperative to opt out, since he still has to be able to get to his
house some way.  And to make the right to opt out one that is
exercisable in practice, there has to be some place that one could
actually *go to*.  (There are lots of people in the world who would
gladly leave their countries, if only some other country would let
them in.)  I suspect that most PPLs would be very selective of whom
they allowed to join.

Would we expect PPLs to have the reverse power to eject members?  That
would be one simple solution to the welfare problem -- all indigent
people will be *thrown out*!

Dale

Dale Worley		Dept. of Math., MIT		drw@math.mit.edu
--
The major cultural hurdle cited by virtually all math-education
experts is the uniquely American idea that only a few gifted people
can do math at all.  This notion is reinforced by the fact that there
are indeed mathematical geniuses.  But, educators argue, making the
assumption that a student who isn't born with the talent of, say,
Isaac Newton can't think mathematically is like saying that nobody
less than Rudolf Serkin can play the piano.  Further, doing well in
math is often social death in American high schools (nobody wants to
be a math nerd).

