Date: Wed, 3 Feb 93 22:29:48 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: ECON/NANO: A short question

D. Owen Rowley presents a view of the value of artifacts that I think
misunderstands an important quirk of human psychology.  He says:

>Take the case of your trilobyte fossil.
>if it is a complete atom for atom copy then there is no difference and it is 
>functionally the same. what difference would it make if it came out of the 
>ground or a replicater. Of course a fossil has litle function outside its 
>appearance , other than to a researcher of fossils. 

But fossil collectors *will* care whether or not they get copies--even ones
that are accurate down to the last molecule.  What they care about is the
chain of causes that brought to coveted item to them, not its functional
attributes in the here-and-now.  Such valuation may be hard for we
info-junkies to understand, but I assure you that it is quite common.  

>the value is no longer in the 
>items uniqueness, but in  the information regarding the form. 

Again, I think this is an inaccurate description of the mindset of an
artifact collector.  Such a person values original artifacts exactly
because they are unique.  Rarity is sought after for its own sake.  Silly? 
You and I may think so, but there are many, many antique collectors who
would disagree.

More charitably, though, I note that there is an information-theoretic
defense for following a strategy of preferring original artifacts.  In
short, such a strategy will tend to improve accuracy of recordation.  Even
strong nanotech copying will introduce some noise in the artifact's
"signal."


