Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 0:11:06 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: PPL: What is a PPL, anyway?

It's not clear to me exactly what you mean when you use PPL,
but I'll go along with your apparent use of it to mean
"jurisdiction", or something close to that.

> But nobody is willing to recognize such entities as PPLs, even
> though they would be private, and they whould contain legal systems.

I'm perfectly willing to do so, and I suspect others are as well.
It's fine by me, in a prescriptive sense, if people set up whatever
unpleasant fascistic PPLs they want, with lifetime membership and
totalitarian laws.  I'll even argue that they should be dealt with
in good faith, if not with good will.  Although such a PPL is
probably more hazardous to bystanders than most (as the Very Special
Leader might decide to start looting), I don't find that sufficient
cause to pre-emptively intervene.

> That element is the practical ability to "opt out" of the
> system at any time, taking with one's self one's property, real
> estate, business, etc.

This option poses problems, and may not be prevalent.  For a PPL
with some unusual laws, in particular: this would let me violate
them, and pick up and leave before this was tied to me.  Other PPLs
might well not be willing to enforce these laws, depending on just
what they are.  Granted, this would be difficult to *stop* in any
case, requiring exit permits and minefields.  However, a requirement
for a "notification period", or a period of joint jurisdiction upon
exit, might be honored by other PPLs.

> And to make the right to opt out one that is exercisable in
> practice, there has to be some place that one could actually *go
> to*.

Geography might be less important.  We do *not* need to have
voluntary states, monolithic in their service package.  De-bundling
and specialization may be a good move for producers and consumers
alike.  Local policing would probably be handled geographically,
perhaps by a restrictive covenant requiring owners to contribute to
a neighborhood fund -- there is a free-rider problem here, no way
around it.  But if I decide that I want a different insurance
company, I can change insurance companies in the usual way.
Likewise, many functions of a protection agency would often be
marketed locally (at least for small companies), but do not require
that neighbors share a provider.

> Would we expect PPLs to have the reverse power to eject members?

Depends on what the contract says.  I wouldn't be willing to place
money on any guess I might make about the prevalent situation.
Guess we'll have to wait and see.

