Date:     Thu, 4 Feb 93 10:20:07 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Patents & Statist Bias

A few words about patents.  According to a libertarian friend who works
for the UC office of technology transfer, where patents are commonplace,
their legal form isn't that of a property right, but a license granting
monopoly franchise upon the "inventor" which empowers them to coerce
others out of the market.  By this interpretation, getting rid of these
would be a great boon to consumers indeed!

Betsy asked about inventors that need to raise lots of money - that's why
we need to keep capital cheap through ending capital gains taxes, income
taxes, hell, all taxes, and relevant regulations.  I'm told that much
of Japan's success is the result of having cheap capital, not from MITI
strategy (BTW, any Bay Area Extropians interested in industrial policy
should go to the next Free Exchange, as that'll be the topic).

Dave Cake noted correctly that the only thing patents do that trade secret
law doesn't is provide information about new inventions to anyone who
wants them.  (Although he seems to think that's why patents were invented.
It's not.  They were invented so that States could keep control of
technology - telescopes - that gave them military superiority over others.)

However, I don't see why inventors shouldn't be able to charge for info
about their inventions without getting compensated, and don't see that they
are by giving away the info they've discovered for free.

Hey, is there a patent on the legal concept of patents :-)?

It also appears we've got another newbie asking questions that appear to
have a statist slant to them.  (I've forgotten his name, though.)  Suffice
to say that statism has a very bad reputation around here.  Michael
Milken is a hero, and Leona Helmsley was a victim of persecution and
scapegoating.

That rhetoric out of the way, what about the middle class, anyways?  There's
lots of statistics about this very complex subject.  Between 1982 and 1990
about 19 million net new jobs came into existence in the USA; between 1988
and 1992, 1.2 million did - 1.1 of them in government;  82-90 saw US GNP
grow by about a third, roughly equal to the size of that of West Germany's
in 82; most of the new jobs paid pretty well; income mobility was high;
in 1980, public-sector jobs paid about as well as their private-sector
equivalents - now, they pay about 10% better.

Also, manufacturing worker productivity went up about 90%, as did US exports.

What's going on?  Women have entered the workforce to the point that most
of them work rather than be housewives - that's who's mostly doing those
19mil. new jobs.  They're doing this not out of feminist sympathy but
because their households have been feeling the pressure of taxes (revenues
from which rose during the 1980s), regulations (Reagan only cut the growth
rate of these, and Bush restored it), and price inflation, which has been
about 3-4% per year.

Manufacturing (now secod to government in total employees per industry for
the first time in US history) has had so many mandated benefits per worker
that they've had to cut staff, automate, and raise the productivity of
the remainder.

Add to this the inflationary effect of a 4 trillion national debt, and
you can see that there's a lot of malinvestment to be liquidated.

And the State keeps rolling merrily along!  The biggest unions in the USA
are now those of public schoolteachers, followed by Public Employees Unions.
Their early contributions to the Clinton campaign gave Billary the
credibility he needed to win.  Their salaries have been rising faster
than their private-sector counterparts, being insulated from market-forces,
and government hiring has only recently been slowed down by the recession
(a.k.a., "Clinton Recovery," in Democratese).  Their parking lots are
filled with brand-spanking new BMWs, Mercedes, Acuras, Infinitys, Lexuses,
etc., while the freeways I drive on have more cars seemingly abandoned
on the side of the road than I remember ever having seen before that have
broken down due to what is called "deferred maintenance" in politicianese.

If Kemp hadn't already won the next Presidential election, if he can
bludgeon the stupid GOP into giving him the nomination, there wouldn't be
much cause for hope at all.

