Date: Thu, 4 Feb 93 11:13:29 PST
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

First, let me offer a definition of zero-sum since Robin wasn't able
to get one from anybody else :-).  A series of transactions is
zero-sum (positive-sum, negative-sum) if the market value of the goods
and cash involved in the transactions, summed over all the
participants, is the same as (greater than, less than) it was when
they started.

Now, with this definition, the futures transaction example I gave is
zero sum.  At the beginning Robin and I had no gold and D dollars; at
the end we have exchanged |450-x| dollars and the net result is we
have no gold and D dollars.

The stock market example, OTOH, is positive sum because the market
value of the stock went up.

Note that whether the market value of gold goes up or down in the
example I gave, we don't have a net profit.  We have simply bet on
what was going to happen.

I know that Robin is at least right when he says that investment
returns should be solely a function of risk.  It is interesting and
surprising to me that commodity returns have historically been in
accordance with this, because commodity investing is very risky, hence
average returns must be quite high.

It seems to me, though, that gambling is an exception to this rule.
If Robin and I bet each other every week on the outcome of a football
game, or which horse will win a race, we won't jointly make any money
on these transactions.  These are zero-sum activities, and they seem
very similar to the futures transaction example I gave.  Gambling is
therefore irrational except for people who make better predictions
than the average gambler.  Nevertheless it is very popular.

Futures transactions seem essentially the same.  I really don't see
how futures transactions, which are basically bets about the future
price of some commodity, or some index, can be profitable for both
participants (except for the case I gave where the participants
actually make or take delivery of the commodity, which is a tiny
fraction of all contracts).  Where does the money come from?

Robin suggests that the example I gave is misleading because a
contract for future delivery at $450 would not have a current "price"
of $450, but would rather be less.  I don't quite understand what is
meant by the "price" of the contract in this sense.  I could see the
contract being in two parts: the "long" position, which Robin has, and
the "short" position, which I have.  If either one of us wanted to get
out of the contract, they would have to pass the contract on to
someone else.  This would in general involve some money changing
hands, too.  In general, it seems to me that the price of the two
sides of the contract would move inversely; for every dollar that the
long position moved up, the short position would lose a dollar in
value.

Robin is right that if we both thought the future price of gold was
$450 we would not enter this contract, since our expected return is 0
and there is considerable risk.  But the more fundamental reason, it
seems to me, is that no two people can enter a contract like this if
they agree on the future price.  There is no way the contract as a
whole can offer a premium to both investors since there is nowhere for
the money to come from.  Like our bet on next week's football game,
whatever Robin loses I will gain.  The only way the contract can occur
is if we disagree on the future price of gold.

If I think the actual future price is $400, and Robin thinks it is
$500, we may both be willing to enter the contract, with both of us
expecting a future return of $50 which we feel is large enough to
compensate us for the risk, in comparison to alternative riskless
returns.

To sum up, most commodity investments seem to work like bets, as Perry
said originally.  Bets are zero-sum transactions, and there is no way
for there to be a net positive return.  I will take a look, as Robin
suggested, at how this is analyzed in the textbooks.  I have always
had the impression that many amateur investors lose a lot of money to
finance the winnings of the professionals.  If Robin is right that
returns averaged over _all_ participants are high, then there must be
more to it than this.  I'd like to know what it is.

Hal

